# Executive summary: who churns, what it costs, and where to spend the retention budget

*IBM Telco Customer Churn sample (real data), {{ k.customers | num }} customers. All figures come from `reports/facts.json`.*

## The problem

{{ k.churn_rate | pct }} of customers ({{ k.churned | num }}) churned. They were paying {{ k.mrr_lost | usd }} a month between them, which is
{{ k.mrr_lost_share | pct }} of monthly revenue and {{ k.annual_revenue_lost | usd }} a year. Churners pay more than the customers who stay
({{ k.avg_charges_churned | money }} vs {{ k.avg_charges_retained | money }} a month), so every lost customer is an above-average one.

## Who churns

- **Short commitments.** Month-to-month customers churn {{ s.m2m.churn_rate | pct(0) }}, {{ s.m2m_vs_two_year_ratio | sig(0) }} times the two-year rate
  ({{ s.two_year.churn_rate | pct(0) }}).
- **New customers.** {{ k.churned_in_first_year_share | pct(0) }} of churners left within their first year; first-year churn is {{ s.tenure_0_12.churn_rate | pct(0) }}.
- **Fiber and electronic check.** Fiber-optic customers churn {{ s.fiber.churn_rate | pct(0) }} and electronic-check payers {{ s.echeck.churn_rate | pct(0) }}.
- **No support services.** Customers without tech support churn {{ s.no_tech_support.churn_rate | pct(0) }} vs {{ s.tech_support.churn_rate | pct(0) }} with it.

The single worst segment is new month-to-month fiber customers: {{ a.revenue_at_risk.hot_share_of_customers | pct(0) }} of the base, {{ a.revenue_at_risk.hot_churn_rate | pct(0) }} churn,
{{ a.revenue_at_risk.hot_share_of_mrr_lost | pct(0) }} of lost revenue.

## What to do

Run a retention offer ({{ ca.offer_discount_pct }}% off the bill for {{ ca.offer_months }} months) for the **{{ g.customers | num }} customers whose predicted churn
risk is at least {{ m.threshold.chosen | pct(0) }}**. If the offer keeps {{ ca.save_rate | pct(0) }} of the would-be churners for a year:

| | Value |
|---|---|
| Revenue kept over {{ ca.value_horizon_months }} months | {{ g.saved_revenue | usd }} |
| Cost of the offer and outreach | {{ g.cost | usd }} |
| **Net value** | **{{ g.net_value | usd }}** (ROI {{ g.roi | pct(0) }}) |
| **Break-even save rate** | **{{ g.breakeven_save_rate | pct }}** |

Targeting everyone would lose {{ (c.groups.everyone.net_value | abs) | usd }}: the discount given to customers who were never going to leave costs more than it saves.
The ranked list is what makes the campaign pay.

## Structural fixes (beyond the campaign)

1. Move month-to-month customers to annual contracts at renewal with a small loyalty discount.
2. Fix the first-year experience for fiber customers (onboarding call, proactive support in month one).
3. Push automatic payment methods; electronic-check payers churn at {{ s.echeck_vs_auto_card_ratio | sig(1) }} times the rate of automatic card payers
   ({{ s.echeck.churn_rate | pct(0) }} vs {{ s.auto_card.churn_rate | pct(0) }}).
4. Bundle tech support and online security into fiber plans.

## Next step

Pilot the offer on a random half of the target list for one quarter, compare churn with the other half, and replace the assumed
{{ ca.save_rate | pct(0) }} save rate with the measured one in `excel/retention_campaign_roi.xlsx`.
